WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.840
Does a probe with 960 recording sites record 960 neurons?

2
00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.200
No. The original Neuropixels probe had that many sites on one shank.

3
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.300
But it could address only 384 at a time.

4
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.820
Two probes in an awake mouse recorded more than 700 well-isolated neurons at once,

5
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.580
from five brain structures.

6
00:00:30.533 --> 00:00:33.133
Neurons are not read off sites.

7
00:00:34.193 --> 00:00:39.013
Spike sorting software groups spikes into units, using the shape of each spike and

8
00:00:39.013 --> 00:00:40.153
the sites where it appears.

9
00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:46.053
One neuron shows up on several neighbouring sites, and one site hears several

10
00:00:46.053 --> 00:00:50.853
neurons. So a unit can merge two neurons, or split one.

11
00:00:51.753 --> 00:00:56.993
Treat each unit as an inference, with quality scores, not a confirmed single neuron.

12
00:00:57.867 --> 00:01:01.787
Now count what you have, in an invented dataset.

13
00:01:02.687 --> 00:01:06.127
Two mice, A and B, each have two sessions.

14
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:09.667
Each unit appears in one session only.

15
00:01:10.667 --> 00:01:15.647
Six units, each watched for four trials, make 24 unit-trials.

16
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:22.247
But units in one mouse share its conditions, so they are not independent observations.

18
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:32.370
Each unit gets a rate in spikes per second: 8, 2, 4, 8, 2 and 2.

19
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.130
Pooled, the rate is 4.33 spikes per second.

20
00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:44.730
But mouse A supplied twice the units of mouse B. Weight each mouse equally, and

21
00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:49.310
mouse A averages 5.5, while mouse B averages 2.

22
00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.050
The animal mean is 3.75 spikes per second.

23
00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:00.930
Count every unit-trial as an independent sample, and n is 24.

24
00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.010
That mistake has a name, pseudoreplication.

25
00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:10.370
For a claim about mice, n is 2, not 24.

26
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:18.050
Many units from one mouse are still one mouse, and the sample size belongs to the level of the question.

28
00:02:18.883 --> 00:02:23.263
Each number here measures something other than what you might assume.

29
00:02:24.423 --> 00:02:27.783
Sites are not channels, and channels are not neurons.

30
00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:33.423
A unit is an inference, and one animal can supply many units.

31
00:02:34.483 --> 00:02:40.363
Report the whole hierarchy: animals, sessions, units and trials.

32
00:02:41.343 --> 00:02:43.543
Then say which level your question is about.
